# 13. AUDIT LOG SECURITY & INTEGRITY

Audit logs must be protected from unauthorized modification.

Requirements:

1. Normal users cannot modify or delete audit events.
2. Agents cannot modify or delete audit events.
3. Audit writing must happen through trusted backend services.
4. Enforce organization-level access when retrieving audit records.
5. Enforce role-based access for audit viewing.
6. Keep security-sensitive audit events protected.
7. Record the actor responsible for privileged changes.
8. Protect audit records from unauthorized application-level updates.
9. Record failures to write required audit events.
10. Never silently continue a sensitive operation when its mandatory audit event cannot be durably recorded.

Where supported by the existing database architecture, use append-only records and appropriate database policies.

A hash chain or cryptographic integrity mechanism may be used if it can be integrated safely with the existing stack. Do not present a hash chain as tamper-proof if privileged database administrators can still rewrite its underlying records.

Define retention and archival behavior using existing organizational and legal requirements. Do not invent arbitrary retention periods.

